---
import Book from '../layouts/Book.astro';
import RunningHead from '../components/RunningHead.astro';
import Sidenote from '../components/Sidenote.astro';
import ProofNote from '../components/ProofNote.astro';
import TurnTheLeaf from '../components/TurnTheLeaf.astro';
import { profile } from '../data/profile';
---
<Book
  title="To the Reader"
  description="The preface to Notes from the Back End: a tech worker whose spirit belongs to a bygone era, free to think and to choose what he is."
  section="preface"
  ribbon="To the Reader"
>
  <article class="leaf has-notes" aria-labelledby="preface-title">
    <RunningHead right="To the Reader" />
    <div class="text">
      <header class="opening">
        <p class="book-label">A Preface</p>
        <h1 id="preface-title">To the Reader</h1>
      </header>

      <p class="preface-line">A fellow <span lang="la">socii malorum</span><Sidenote n={1}>Schopenhauer proposed that the fitting way to address one another is not “Sir” or “Monsieur” but “fellow-sufferer”: <i lang="la">socî malorum</i>, <i lang="fr">compagnon de misères</i>.</Sidenote> and cognitariat,<Sidenote n={2}>The cognitariat: in later Marxist writing, the proletariat of knowledge workers, who sell their minds by the hour as the factory hand once sold his arms.</Sidenote> tech-savvy literary romanticist.</p>

      <div class="prose">
        <p>That is the line I put on every profile, and this book is an attempt to explain it. I earn my living as a back-end engineer in Bogor, building the parts of software that nobody sees. I give the rest of my attention to Dostoevsky and Tolstoy, to Nietzsche, Marx and Schopenhauer, and, beside them, to Shakespeare.</p>
        <p>I may be a tech worker, but my spirit belongs to a bygone era. That is not nostalgia. It is a choice, and the choosing is the point: I am free to think, and free to decide what I am. An old German song says it better than I can: <i lang="de">Die Gedanken sind frei.</i> Thoughts are free.</p>
        <p>So I have bound my work as a book. The Chronicle, Book I, records where I have worked and what I built there. The Notes, Book II, look closely at a few pieces of that work, as both an engineer and a reader would want to. The Tree of Knowledge, Book III, lists my instruments, each with a reference to the place where I used it. <i lang="de">Gedanken</i>, Book IV, is for my verses, aphorisms and critiques.</p>
        <p>Read it in order, or open it anywhere. Either way, read slowly.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="signature">
        <span class="signature-name">{profile.name}</span>
        <svg viewBox="0 0 220 30" aria-hidden="true"><path class="flourish" d="M6,18 C40,4 70,28 104,16 S168,6 214,14"></path></svg>
        <span class="signature-place">Bogor, West Java</span>
      </div>

      <ProofNote>A first draft from the author’s tagline and motto. The author’s own words are to replace it.</ProofNote>

      <TurnTheLeaf prev={{ path: '/#contents', label: 'Contents' }} next={{ path: '/chronicle/', label: 'Book I, The Chronicle' }} />
    </div>
  </article>
</Book>
